  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:35:36.321Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSection 51-A of the NIRC provides that individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, where the income tax has been correctly withheld, are not required to file an annual income tax return. The certificate of withholding from the employer serves as the substituted filing of the income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nThis section simplifies the tax compliance process for employees who receive purely compensation income from a single employer. It alleviates the burden of filing an annual income tax return for those whose tax obligations have already been satisfied through withholding by their employer. The employer's certificate of withholding, which indicates that the tax due equals the tax withheld, is sufficient for compliance.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor employees, this means less paperwork and a streamlined process, as they do not need to file a separate income tax return if their tax has been withheld correctly. Employers must ensure that they provide the necessary certificates to their employees, which will serve as proof of tax compliance. This provision aims to enhance taxpayer convenience and reduce administrative burdens on both employees and the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).",
